@page "/"
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]

<PageTitle>Signalboard</PageTitle>

<h1>Two tenants that cannot see each other</h1>

<p>
    Signalboard is a sample product built on Lakewright.NET. It exists to make one claim checkable:
    a customer of one organization cannot reach another organization's work, and cannot learn that
    the work exists.
</p>

<h2>Who you can be</h2>

<table>
    <thead>
        <tr><th>Organization</th><th>Person</th><th>Role</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td rowspan="2">Acme Logistics</td><td>Alice</td><td>Admin</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Vera</td><td>Viewer</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Globex Freight</td><td>Bob</td><td>Admin</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p>
    <a class="cta" href="/signin">Sign in as one of them</a>
</p>

<h2>What to try</h2>

<p>
    Sign in as Alice and start an operation. Sign in as Bob and open the same operation's address:
    you get <strong>404, not 403</strong>. A 403 would confirm the operation exists, which is a
    smaller leak than reading it but a leak all the same. Bob is not a member of Acme, so the tenant
    never resolves and the request stops before authorization is consulted.
</p>

<p>
    Then sign in as Vera. She is a member of Acme, so the tenant resolves and she can read. Starting
    work gets her a 403, because a Viewer is below the role that endpoint requires. Two denials, two
    status codes, two different reasons.
</p>

<h2>The same thing from a terminal</h2>

<p>
    The HTTP API is the same one the dashboard uses. Authenticate with a header instead of a cookie:
</p>

<pre><code>curl -i -X POST http://localhost:8080/organizations/@(DemoTenants.Acme.Value)/operations \
  -H "X-Demo-User: @DemoTenants.Alice" \
  -H "Idempotency-Key: $(uuidgen)" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"kind":"analysis"}'</code></pre>

<p>
    Send the same <code>Idempotency-Key</code> twice and you get the same operation back rather than
    a second Databricks run. Reuse it with a different <code>kind</code> and you get 422, because
    answering with the stored operation would answer a question you did not ask.
</p>

<p>
    Read <a href="/openapi/v1.json">the OpenAPI document</a> for the rest.
</p>

<h2>What is not real here</h2>

<p>
    Authentication. Signing in picks a name from a list and trusts it, so that the sample runs with
    one container and no identity provider. That is safe to demonstrate with precisely because
    isolation does not depend on it: membership is read from the database on every request, so
    claiming to be Bob gets you Bob's access and nothing more.
</p>

<p>
    Without a Databricks workspace configured no worker starts, so operations stay
    <code>Pending</code>. Nothing here fakes a run.
</p>
